    </figure>

    <aside class="not-prose mx-auto my-10 max-w-3xl rounded-2xl border-l-4 border-terracotta/60 bg-terracotta/5 p-6">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-terracotta-dark mb-2">From the gym floor</p>
      <p class="text-text-primary leading-relaxed">The fittest people past seventy I have ever worked with did not share a macro ratio either. One cooked the way her mother had in Greece: olive oil, greens, beans, a table full of people. One ate the way her family always had: rice, vegetables, soy, small plates. They shared two things, a kitchen somebody actually cooked in and bodies that moved nearly every day. When I finally read the longevity research, I recognised the pattern before I finished the first page.</p>
    </aside>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[68ch] space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-primary">
      <p>Not a single healthy long-lived population ran on ultra-processed food. Not one ran on a diet that offered nothing to the body or to the people eating it. If macro ratios were the key, Okinawa and Ikaria could not share a planet.</p>
    </div>

    <aside class="not-prose mx-auto my-10 max-w-3xl rounded-2xl border border-sage/40 bg-sage/5 px-6 py-5 sm:px-8">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-sage-dark mb-2">In short</p>
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-primary">The long-lived populations disagree on macros and agree on food quality, daily movement and a kitchen someone actually cooks in.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>

