  <section id="judge" class="container-custom max-w-4xl scroll-mt-36 py-10 md:py-16 bg-cream-dark rounded-3xl">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-text-primary mb-4">How to judge any diet in seven questions</h2>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[68ch] space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-primary mb-8">
      <p>Every plan I have handed a client had to survive these seven questions first. They are also the checklist behind each guide above. It takes about two minutes to run through any plan a book, influencer, GP or app hands you. If a plan survives all seven, it will probably work, whatever it is called.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="not-prose mx-auto mb-10 max-w-3xl">
      <img src="/images/diets/article/ingredients-label.webp" alt="Close-up of a snack packet ingredients label listing maize, vegetable oils, cheese powder, flavour enhancers (E621, E627, E631), colours and emulsifiers, with a nutrition table per 100 g and per 30 g serving." width="1600" height="1067" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="w-full h-auto rounded-2xl border border-border shadow-sm" />
      <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">Illustration: a typical ultra-processed ingredients panel. Maize, oils and cheese powder first, then flavour enhancers (E621, E627, E631), colours and emulsifiers. If the list is longer than the food, treat that as a signal.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <ol class="mx-auto space-y-4 max-w-3xl">
      {[
        ['Does it run on whole food?', 'Look at what you would actually eat each day, not the marketing. If about 80% of it is recognisable food, that is a good start. If it depends on branded bars, shakes or powders, it is a product business wearing a diet costume.'],
        ['Does it cover protein?', 'Around 0.8 g/kg body weight minimum for healthy adults, and more for older adults, hard training or a calorie deficit. Any diet that cannot get you there is a weight plan first and a health plan second.'],
        ['Does it cover fibre and micronutrients?', 'Check where the plants, whole grains, nuts, fruit and legumes sit in the plan. Total elimination of a whole food family needs a reason and usually a supplement.'],
        ['Is the energy balance survivable?', 'Portions should land near what you burn. If the plan requires misery to maintain a deficit, you will regain and blame yourself rather than the plan.'],
        ['What are you being asked to eliminate, and why?', 'Ultra-processed food earns elimination with the most consistent evidence in nutrition. Food families like grains, dairy or plants get no such high ground. Legitimate arguments exist, but the burden of proof is on the eliminator.'],
        ['What is the evidence grade on the actual claim?', 'Not anecdotes. Not testimonials. Randomized trials, cohorts or, better, a meta-analysis. This page explains the grades, and every guide shows its own.'],
        ['Would you still be doing it in five years?', 'The most underused question in nutrition. Over twelve-month horizons nearly every sensible plan converges; over five years, only the sustainable ones have members.']
      ].map(([q, a], i) => (
        <li class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-border p-5 shadow-sm">
          <p class="font-bold text-text-primary flex gap-3"><span class="text-sage">{i + 1}.</span> {q}</p>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">{a}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="limits" class="container-custom max-w-4xl scroll-mt-36 py-10 md:py-16">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-text-primary mb-4">When a diet is not okay</h2>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[68ch] space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-primary mb-8">
      <p>I have also met people who found the edge of "any sensible diet can work" the hard way. Some of them were on plans I would run differently today, back when I had not yet learned where the fence was. So read this section slowly. Some patterns are not sensible as written. Some people should not restrict without a clinician involved. And a few red flags cancel out anything else a website claims, whatever credentials stand behind it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-border bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
        <p class="font-bold text-text-primary mb-2">Red flags in the plan itself</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2.5 text-text-secondary text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-error font-bold" aria-hidden="true">×</span>Bans an entire food group without a medical reason and expects you to buy their supplement line</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-error font-bold" aria-hidden="true">×</span>Claims to work for everyone, always, and dismisses real risks as myths</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-error font-bold" aria-hidden="true">×</span>Sells the "one true diet" and treats every alternative as sabotage or corruption</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-error font-bold" aria-hidden="true">×</span>Promises more than 2 lbs a week of fat loss, indefinitely, or "cures" a chronic disease</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-error font-bold" aria-hidden="true">×</span>Cites no studies, or cites only testimonials, or cannot tell you what its own risks are</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-border bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
        <p class="font-bold text-text-primary mb-2">The people who should not self-restrict</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2.5 text-text-secondary text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-terracotta-dark font-bold" aria-hidden="true">!</span>Anyone with type 1 diabetes, kidney disease, heart failure, or a complex medication regimen. Dietary change can change drug doses.</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-terracotta-dark font-bold" aria-hidden="true">!</span>Anyone pregnant or breastfeeding. Restriction has a real cost curve.</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-terracotta-dark font-bold" aria-hidden="true">!</span>Anyone with a personal or family history of disordered eating. Rigid food rules reliably escalate.</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-terracotta-dark font-bold" aria-hidden="true">!</span>Children and teenagers, whose nutritional floors are higher and whose relationships with food are still developing.</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-terracotta-dark font-bold" aria-hidden="true">!</span>Older adults under-eating protein. Sarcopenia hides in calorie deficits.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mx-auto mt-8 max-w-[68ch] space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-primary">
      <p>Where does that leave the strictest patterns: carnivore, 80/10/10 and macrobiotic? We cover them anyway, in full, with their evidence grade on the first screen rather than buried nine scrolls down. Read <a href="/diets/carnivore" class="text-sage-dark hover:text-sage font-medium underline decoration-sage/40 underline-offset-2">the carnivore guide</a> or <a href="/diets/80-10-10" class="text-sage-dark hover:text-sage font-medium underline decoration-sage/40 underline-offset-2">the 80/10/10 guide</a> and you will see the "any sensible diet can work" idea does not need to stretch to cover the extreme versions. It simply cannot stretch that far, and this hub says so.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="method" class="container-custom max-w-6xl scroll-mt-36 py-10 md:py-16 bg-white border-y border-border">
    <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-10">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-text-primary mb-4">How we research the diet guides</h2>
        <p class="text-text-primary leading-relaxed mb-4">The gym floor taught me what to look for. This pipeline is how every guide earns its verdict, and the outcome grades get published up front rather than buried in the eleventh paragraph.</p>
        <ol class="space-y-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sage font-bold">1</span><span><strong class="text-text-primary">Source the primary literature.</strong> Randomized trials, systematic reviews, large prospective cohorts and meta-analyses, with DOIs so you can verify every claim in one click.</span></li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sage font-bold">2</span><span><strong class="text-text-primary">Grade the evidence</strong> A through D, domain by domain, on a standard rubric adapted from GRADE. Not a vibe.</span></li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sage font-bold">3</span><span><strong class="text-text-primary">Write for the reader, not the exam.</strong> Plain sentences, real numbers, and mechanistic jargon translated on first use.</span></li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sage font-bold">4</span><span><strong class="text-text-primary">Separate food from the label.</strong> "Clean" and "dirty" versions are explicitly addressed for every pattern that has them.</span></li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sage font-bold">5</span><span><strong class="text-text-primary">Publish the risks with the verdict.</strong> Every guide carries a "who should not do this" section and a firm not-medical-advice notice.</span></li>
